Date: Mon, 17 Jan 94 01:13:50 EST
From: trestrab@GVSU.EDU (BETH TRESTRAIL)
Subject: Reputations & anon business

I traded precious metals over networked systems for 8 years, with
individual transactions running up into 6 digits. Most of the people
I did business with were completely unknown to me except by
reputation; we would know each others voices, but I couldn't
have picked them out of a police lineup to save my life. I also did
large amounts of business with individuals locally on an anonymous
basis (this was before IRS cash reporting regulations were in effect).
Some nyms had credit lines as high as 6 figures; most were on a
strict cash and carry basis. The reputation of an individual was the
paramount factor in determining the level of risk you were
exposing yourself to when dealing with them. The perceived
risk associated with a given individual or firm determined
their credit levels _at a given point in time_. Because the
metals market was a low profit-margin, highly volatile
business, correctly figuring your risk made the difference
between turning a profit and losing your shirt.

Because people's ability (and willingness) to uphold their part of
a bargain could change - often overnight, you had to keep up to date
on anything that might affect the risks you would encounter. This
included using "intuition" to make judgements concerning behavior that
was out of the norm. For example, if dealer A was all of a sudden
making offers to buy goods far in excess of his normal levels, one
had to decide (sometimes in seconds) if he had hit upon a hot streak
and was doing very well or he was in trouble and perhaps thinking of
burning you and financing his new career with your hard earned money.

Your reputation with a particular dealer was a combination of his
direct personal dealings with you and his take on what others, whose
opinion he trusted, thought of you. The "web of trust" model
dominated the marketplace; there were no real central information
clearing houses like TRW and others operate for consumer credit.

The jewelry industry runs in much the same fashion, although the
Jewelers Board of Trade maintains credit listings on many. Reputation
carries more weight than credit files, though; its easier to get
a business relationship started and credit established with an
appropriate introduction than with a reference to your credit
listing in the JBT Red book (a listing of jewelers and their
credit ratings published semi-annually).

          Jeff Trestrail
          trestrab@gvsu.edu

