>in this definition.  For example, relationships which are not based on
>sex, such as those between family members, coworkers, or members of an
>organization (see below) may also be Extropian.

My first guess is that that these are different kinds of relationships than
Geoff was talking about.  Since the topic was "Extropians in Love", I think
romantic relationships are what are at issue.  Not that other kinds of
relationships aren't important, or even that they won't fit into the
framework being constructed, but I'm suggesting that it may be simpler to
break down the problem and consider the types of relationships separately
(romantic, friendly, familial, business, etc.), then look for possible
unifying factors -- a process of decomposition, analysis, and synthesis --
than to consider all kinds at once.

Rather than using a
>rejection of the cultural norm as a basis, I might suggest an
>embracing of the Extropian Principles (v2.5), either wholely or in
>part, as a criterion.  Note, however, that I would *not* include a
>spiritual relationship, such as that between a Nun and the Holy Ghost
>(or any of its non-Christian cultural variants) as such a
>relationship, as it (and religion in general) is *not* rational, nor
>is it (in the case of organized religions) self-determined by the
>individual.

I think it might be useful to consider such relationships.  I'd say the nun
is doing the reasonable thing based on her heartfelt convictions and her
assessments of the consequences of her actions, however she might have come
by those convictions and assessments.  The relationship is self-determined
at least insofar as the concept of "personal relationship with God" is
present and the nun has the freedom to leave the convent.

In this as well as other areas of this discussion, it might be useful to
include rather than exclude, and if we can't find a category for something,
make a new category, rather than tossing the whole thing out.  As my friend
Jim says:  "A pile for everything, and everything in its pile!".

In general, I'd say (can you guess?) communication is paramount.  After
that, mutual attraction and shared values.

I don't think that the "marriage is forever" concept fits well with
Extropian relationships.  Self-Transformation dictates that the parties
involved in the relationship will change, and not necessarily in the same
directions.  In the future, transformation will occur much more quickly
than it does now.  Maybe a relationship could last a century, or even a
millenium, but 10,000 years?  100,000?  On the contrary, I'd actually
expect relationships to be shifting almost constantly, and/or not to be
exclusive.  Time enough for love.

Kennita




